#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Compute the ground state energy of a hydrogen atom with VQE {#compute-the-"
"ground-state-energy-of-a-hydrogen-atom-with-vqe}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Now, let's use what we've learned to compute the ground state energy of a "
"hydrogen atom. Throughout the module, we will use a framework for quantum "
"computing known as \"Qiskit patterns\", which breaks down workflows into the"
" following steps:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Step 1: Map classical inputs to a quantum problem\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Step 2: Optimize problem for quantum execution\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Step 3: Execute using Qiskit Runtime primitives\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Step 4: Post-processing and classical analysis\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Qiskit pattern](/learning/images/modules/computer-"
"science/vqe/patterns.svg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "We will generally follow these steps.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Let's start by loading some necessary packages, including Qiskit Runtime "
"primitives. We will also select the least busy quantum computer available to"
" us.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"There is code below for saving your credentials upon first use. Be sure to "
"delete this information from the notebook after saving it to your "
"environment, so that your credentials are not accidentally shared when you "
"share the notebook. See [Set up your IBM Cloud account](/guides/initialize-"
"account) and [Initialize the service in an untrusted "
"environment](/guides/cloud-setup-untrusted) for more guidance.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The cell below will allow you to switch between using the simulator or real "
"hardware throughout the notebook. We recommend running it now:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 1: Map the problem to quantum circuits and operators {#step-1-map-the-"
"problem-to-quantum-circuits-and-operators}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We start our VQE calculation by defining the Hamiltonian for the hydrogen "
"molecule ($H_2$) at a specific bond distance. This Hamiltonian represents "
"the total energy of the system in terms of qubit operators, having been "
"produced and mapped from the molecular system using a standard procedure: 1)"
" employing the STO-6G basis set (a specific collection of mathematical "
"functions used to approximate the electron orbitals), 2) applying the "
"Jordan-Wigner mapping (a technique to translate fermionic operators "
"describing electrons into qubit operators), and 3) performing qubit "
"reduction using parity of the Hamiltonian to simplify the problem.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"As we previously explained, the computed ground state energies depend "
"heavily on the basis set selection and the molecular geometry (like bond "
"distance). For this specific configuration and after these transformations, "
"the resulting qubit Hamiltonian is simple:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "$$\\hat{H} = -0.2355 I + 0.2355 Z$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Here, $I$ represents the identity operator and $Z$ represents the Pauli-Z "
"operator, acting on a single qubit. The coefficients are derived from the "
"integrals calculated using the STO-6G basis set at this particular bond "
"distance with proper transformation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"With this Hamiltonian defined, we can now use VQE to compute its ground "
"state energy. It is useful to compare our calculated ground state energy to "
"expected values. For a single, isolated hydrogen atom (H), the ground state "
"energy is exactly -0.5 Hartree (in the absence of relativistic effects). Let"
" us compute the exact ground state energy of *our specific qubit "
"Hamiltonian* as defined above and compare it to relevant known values.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Next, we need a parameterized quantum circuit, an ansatz, to prepare a trial"
" wave function $\\Psi_\\text{trial}$ for the ground state. The goal is to "
"find the parameters $\\theta$ that minimize the energy expectation value "
"$\\langle\\psi(\\theta)|\\hat{H}|\\psi(\\theta)\\rangle$. The choice of "
"ansatz is crucial because it determines the set of possible quantum states "
"that our circuit can prepare. A \"good\" ansatz is one that is flexible "
"enough to represent a state very close to the true ground state of the "
"Hamiltonian we are studying, but not so complex that it requires too many "
"parameters or too deep a circuit for current quantum computers.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Here, we will try three different one-qubit ansätze to see which one "
"provides better \"coverage\" of the possible quantum states a single qubit "
"can be in. The \"coverage\" refers to the range of quantum states that the "
"ansatz circuit can produce by varying its parameters.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We will use three ansätze based on different combinations of single-qubit "
"rotational gates:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"One 1-axis rotational gate ansatz: This ansatz uses rotations around only a "
"single axis ($R_x(\\theta)$). On the Bloch sphere, this corresponds to "
"moving only along a specific circle. This is the least flexible and covers a"
" limited set of states.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Two 2-axis rotational gate ansätze: These ansätze combines rotations around "
"two different axes ($R_x(\\theta_1) R_z(\\theta_2)$ and $R_x(\\theta_1) "
"R_z(\\theta_2) R_x(\\theta_3)$). This allows us to reach a larger portion of"
" the Bloch sphere, compared to a single-axis rotation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"By comparing the VQE results obtained with these three ansätze, we can see "
"how the flexibility and state-space coverage of the ansatz impact our "
"ability to find the true ground state energy of our simplified Hamiltonian. "
"A more flexible ansatz has the *potential* to find a better approximation, "
"but it might also be harder for the classical optimizer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Now, let's generate 5000 random numbers for each parameter and plot the "
"distribution of random quantum states, generated by the three ansätze with "
"these random parameters. You can think of these parameters like rotations "
"around different axes on a spherical surface. To see the distribution of "
"quantum state, we will use [the Bloch "
"Sphere](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bloch_sphere), a three-dimensional "
"sphere that shows the state of a single qubit. Any point on the sphere "
"represents a possible state of the qubit, where the north and south poles "
"are like the classical \"0\" and \"1\", but the qubit can also be anywhere "
"in between, showing special quantum properties like superposition. First, "
"prepare the necessary functions to plot the 3D Bloch sphere and prepare 5000"
" random parameters.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Let's see how our first ansatz works.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/modules/computer-"
"science/vqe/extracted-outputs/f7d886a0-d410-418d-a7a3-81a9134cbfd0-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We can see our first ansatz returns a ring-shaped distributed quantum states"
" of the Bloch sphere. This makes sense, because we have only given the "
"ansatz a single rotational parameter. It can therefore only produce states "
"rotated around one axis. Starting from the point $(0,0,1)$ and rotating "
"around one axis will always yield a ring. Then let's check our second "
"ansatz, which has two orthogonal rotational gates - `Rx` and `Rz`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/modules/computer-"
"science/vqe/extracted-outputs/607e4c0f-cb14-4036-901b-021cc8e11b79-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Here, we can see that our second ansatz covers a larger portion of the Bloch"
" sphere - but note that the dots are more concentrated around the poles and "
"more spread out around the equator. Now it is time to check our last "
"ansatz.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/modules/computer-"
"science/vqe/extracted-outputs/21ff021a-7cfe-4ba9-a827-edac71c0871c-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Here you can see more evenly distributed quantum states generated by our "
"last ansatz.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"As mentioned, the best thing to do is to gain knowledge about the ground "
"state you're seeking and use an ansatz that is well-suited to probe states "
"close to that ground state. For example, if we knew that our ground state "
"was near a pole, we might select ansatz 2. For simplicity, we will stick "
"with ansatz 3, which uniformly probes the entire Bloch sphere.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Now that we have selected our ansatz, let's draw the circuit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/modules/computer-"
"science/vqe/extracted-outputs/20221e72-51dc-49d9-8a09-9b64de7f535f-1.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 2: Optimize for target hardware {#step-2-optimize-for-target-hardware}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"When running a calculation on a real quantum computer, we don't just care "
"about the logic of the quantum circuit. We also care about things like what "
"operations can be performed by that particular quantum computer, and where "
"on the quantum computer are the qubits we are using. Are they right next to "
"each other? Are they far apart? Therefore, the next step is to rewrite our "
"circuit using gates that are natural for the quantum computer we'll use, and"
" taking qubit layout into account. This can be done by `transpilation` - "
"after this process, you can see our simple ansatz converted into a different"
" set of gates, and our abstract qubits will be mapped into physical qubits "
"on a real quantum computer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/modules/computer-"
"science/vqe/extracted-outputs/b5e08232-d651-4df4-bcb7-533513144eff-1.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"You can see the `rx, rz` gates of our ansatz were converted into a series of"
" `rz, sx` gates, which are the native gates of our backend. Also, you can "
"see our `q0` is now mapped into the fifth physical qubit. We also need to "
"map our Hamiltonian according to these changes, as in the following code:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 3: Execute on target hardware {#step-3-execute-on-target-hardware}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Now it is time to run our VQE on a real QPU. For this, first we need a cost "
"function for the optimization process, which evaluates the expectation value"
" of the Hamiltonian with a quantum state, generated by the ansatz. Don't "
"worry! You don't need to code everything by yourself. We prepared a function"
" for this, and all you need to do is run the cell below.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Finally, we prepare initial parameters for our ansatz and its optimization "
"process. You can simply use all zeroes or random values. We have selected "
"initial parameters below, but feel free to comment or uncomment lines in the"
" cell to sample parameters randomly, uniformly from 0 to $2\\pi$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Congratulations! You have just finished your first quantum chemistry "
"experiment successfully. We can see a difference between the exact ground "
"state energy of the Hamiltonian and ours, but because we used a default "
"error mitigation technique (which corrects readout errors), the difference "
"is minor. This is a very good start!\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Note: You can get a better result by setting a level of error mitigation "
"using [`resilience_level`](/guides/error-mitigation-and-suppression-"
"techniques). The default value is 1, and if you set a higher value, it will "
"use more QPU time but might return a better result.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Step 4: Post-process {#step-4-post-process}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"It is time to take a look at how our classical optimizer worked. Run the "
"cell below and see the convergence pattern.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/modules/computer-"
"science/vqe/extracted-outputs/bee036cf-1255-4b4c-881b-cae6e903e09a-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We started with a fairly good initial value, such that we obtained a good "
"final value in just 10 steps. You can see big and small peaks, and this is "
"the typical feature of the COBYLA optimizer - it searches the space as if it"
" cannot see the landscape and adjusts step sizes with each measurement.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding-3}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"What is your observation? Which part of the above process is open to "
"improvement in order to obtain results closer to the theoretical values, or "
"closer to the precise ground state energy of the Hamiltonian? What are some "
"things to consider for this?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The first thing to consider is the change in the set of bases used in "
"calculating the Hamiltonian of molecules. As mentioned earlier, the ground "
"state energy of the H atom is -0.5 Hartree, as is well known, and the STO-6G"
" basis we have chosen is not enough to accurately derive this value.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Choosing a more complex kind of basis increases the number of qubits used by"
" the Hamiltonian; therefore, we need to select a more complex and suitable "
"ansatz for chemistry problems.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The next to be optimized is the management of noise in the QPU. More "
"advanced error mitigation techniques yield better results but may take "
"longer to use. Also, consider how the `shot_number` affects the results.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Finally, better convergence performance can also be achieved by trying "
"different optimizers.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Compute the ground state energy of the hydrogen molecule with VQE {#compute-"
"the-ground-state-energy-of-the-hydrogen-molecule-with-vqe}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Now that we have looked at the overall process of VQE using $H$ atoms, we "
"will now calculate the ground state energy of the $H_2$ molecule more "
"quickly.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 1: Map the problem to quantum circuits and operators {#step-1-map-the-"
"problem-to-quantum-circuits-and-operators-1}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Here we also provide you with a one-qubit Hamiltonian that uses the STO-6G "
"basis and the Jordan-Wigner transformation, with qubit reduction by using a "
"symmetry of the Hamiltonian. Note that we used an atomic distance between "
"two hydrogen atoms of `0.735` $\\mathring A$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Unlike the calculation of a single hydrogen atom ($H$), in order to compute the ground state of a hydrogen molecule($H_2$), we must also consider the repulsive force acting between the nuclei of the two hydrogen atoms, in addition to the energy associated with the electronic orbitals. In this step, we will give this value as a constant, and we will actually calculate this value in the check-in problem.\n"
"$$\\hat{H} = -1.04886 I + -0.79674 Z + 0.18122 X$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 2: Optimize for target hardware {#step-2-optimize-for-target-"
"hardware-1}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Since the number of qubits used by the previous VQE and Hamiltonian is the "
"same as the backend to be used for execution, we will use the existing "
"ansatz and its optimized form.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 3: Execute on target hardware {#step-3-execute-on-target-hardware-1}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Now it's time to do the calculations on the actual QPU. Almost everything is"
" the same, but we will use the appropriate initial point to fit the "
"Hamiltonian. Also, at an iterative part, some of the settings of the "
"`Estimator`, which is used to calculate the Hamiltonian's expectations for "
"the ansatz in the QPU, will be set slightly differently from the previous "
"calculations. We will discuss this change further in a check-in question.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Despite VQE theoretically providing an upper bound to the true ground state "
"energy, practical implementations on real or noisy simulated quantum "
"hardware, as well as approximations made in preparing the Hamiltonian (like "
"basis sets or qubit reduction), can introduce errors that sometimes result "
"in a measured energy slightly lower than the exact theoretical value or a "
"specific numerical reference. Although there are some errors, the results "
"seem to be satisfactory, especially given the small number of steps. Now, "
"let's finish this VQE calculation by looking at how the optimizer worked.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Step 4: Post-process {#step-4-post-process-1}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/modules/computer-"
"science/vqe/extracted-outputs/4b82b644-e030-4983-9fd3-83c3e9dbf307-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding-4}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Let's compute the nuclear repulsion energy of $H_2$ molecule, which we "
"included as a constant value (0.71997 Hartree).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![H2 molecule](/learning/images/modules/computer-science/vqe/h2.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Please use [Coulomb law](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coulomb%27s_law) and "
"[atomic unit](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atomic_units) to make sure you "
"get the value in `Hartree`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Since both hydrogen nuclei are positively charged, they repel each other due"
" to electrostatic force. This repulsion is described by Coulomb's law:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "$$E_{repulsive} = \\frac{e^2}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0R}$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"where $e$ is a charge of proton, $\\epsilon_0$ is a vacuum permittivity, and"
" $R$ is the distance between the two nuclei, measured in meters or Bohr "
"radii in unit of joules(J).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To compute this energy in Hartrees, we need to convert the above equation "
"into the Atomic Unit (AU) system. In AU, $e^2 = 1$, $4\\pi\\epsilon_0=1$ and"
" Bohr radius ($a_0$) is 1 and becomes the fundamental length scale in AU. "
"With these simplifications, Coulomb's law reduces to:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "$$E_{repulsion} = \\frac{1}{R}$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "where $R$ must be measured in Bohr radii ($a_0$).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To convert the given nuclear separation in $\\r{A}$ into $a_0$, we need this"
" conversion relation:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "$$1\\r{A} = 1.88973 a_0$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "so $0.735\\r{A}$ becomes $0.735 * 1.88973 = 1.38895 a_0$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Therefore, the nuclear repulsion energy of a given $H_2$ is\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "$$E_{repulsion} = \\frac{1}{R} = \\frac{1}{1.38895} = 0.71997 Hartree$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Compute reaction energy of $H + H = H_2$ {#compute-reaction-energy-of-h-"
"h-h-2}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Now let's use what we've obtained! You've used VQE, a variational quantum "
"eigensolver, to calculate the ground state energy of the $H$ atom and of the"
" $H_2$ molecule. What's left is to use the calculated values to get the "
"reaction energy of the $H+H=H_2$ process.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Reaction energy is the energy change that happens when substances react to "
"form new substances. Imagine you are building something: sometimes you need "
"to put energy into it (like stacking blocks), and sometimes energy is "
"released (like a ball rolling downhill). In chemistry, reactions either "
"absorb energy (endothermic) or release energy (exothermic).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The reaction energy of $H+H = H_2$ process can be compute by the following "
"formula:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "$E_{reaction} = E_{H_2} - (E_H + E_H)$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"By running the cell below, let's see this visually. Here we will use the "
"exact ground state value of each Hamiltonian, and we'll compare the reaction"
" energy of the exact solution and VQE results.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/modules/computer-"
"science/vqe/extracted-outputs/87e1e660-ae81-43f4-adbe-a229b9d9c889-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"As shown in the figure, although there are some errors, the exact ground "
"state energy of Hamiltonians and the reaction energy calculated using the "
"VQE results are similar, close to -0.2 Hartree.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"It should be noted here that the reaction energy of this process has a negative value, which means that the energy is released through the process, and the resulting molecule has a lower energy than two single atoms.\n"
"6. Conclusion\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Let's summarize what we've learned so far.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We first looked at two important approximation techniques needed to solve "
"quantum chemistry problems: the variational principle and basis set choices,"
" which are both fundamental to VQE. We explored the variational principle by"
" hand, calculating the ground state energy of the simple harmonic "
"oscillator.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Next, we explored VQE, a widely-used algorithm for calculating the ground "
"state energy of a quantum system. We ran code to calculate the ground state "
"energies for atomic hydrogen ($H$) and the hydrogen molecule ($H_2$). In "
"particular, we learned that it is necessary to obtain the appropriate "
"molecular Hamiltonian for the system and to transform it into a form "
"executable on a quantum computer. We also saw that the ansatz, a "
"parameterized quantum circuit, is needed to prepare trial quantum states "
"within VQE, and we discussed the importance of choosing an appropriate "
"ansatz circuit structure. We also learned that VQE relies on an iterative "
"optimization process using a classical computer, guiding the quantum circuit"
" to find the lowest energy state, and saw how the process converges.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Finally, we used the computed ground state energies of $H$ and $H_2$ "
"obtained through VQE to calculate the reaction energy for the process $H + H"
" \\rightarrow H_2$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"VQE is a powerful near-term quantum algorithm, but it's important to be "
"aware of its limitations. The performance of VQE heavily depends on the "
"choice of the ansatz – finding an efficiently preparable ansatz that can "
"accurately represent the true ground state becomes challenging for larger, "
"more complex molecules. Furthermore, current quantum hardware is susceptible"
" to noise, which can impact the accuracy of VQE results, particularly for "
"deeper circuits or larger numbers of qubits. Despite these challenges, VQE "
"serves as a foundational algorithm, and ongoing research is exploring more "
"sophisticated variational methods and error mitigation techniques to push "
"the boundaries of what is possible in quantum chemistry on near-term quantum"
" computers. For instance, algorithms like Sample-based Quantum "
"Diagonalization (SQD) are being developed, which leverage samples obtained "
"from quantum circuits combined with classical diagonalization in a subspace "
"to improve energy estimation and address some of the limitations faced by "
"VQE, particularly regarding measurement efficiency and noise robustness.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Review and questions {#review-and-questions}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Critical concepts: {#critical-concepts}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Variational quantum algorithm is a computing paradigm in which a classical "
"computer and a quantum computer work together to solve a problem.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In VQE, we start with a Hamiltonian of our system and map it onto qubits for"
" execution on the quantum computer. We select a parameterized quantum "
"circuit, an ansatz, and make repeated measurements, varying the parameters "
"of the ansatz, until the lowest energy value is reached. The search through "
"parameter space is done using a classical optimizer. To achieve good "
"results, it is necessary to select a good ansatz and an appropriate "
"optimizer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Reaction energy is the total energy change in a chemical reaction, "
"determined by the difference between the energy of the reactants and the "
"products.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "True/false {#truefalse}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The variational principle states that the expectation value of the energy "
"for any trial wave function is always greater than or equal to the true "
"ground state energy.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A basis set is a collection of functions used to approximate quantum wave "
"functions.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '3. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"VQE is a quantum algorithm used to exactly solve the Schrödinger equation "
"for a given Hamiltonian.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '4. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In VQE, a parameterized quantum circuit (an ansatz) is used to prepare trial"
" wave functions.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '5. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The choice of optimizer in VQE (for example, COBYLA, SPSA, or ADAM) does not"
" impact the quality of the result.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '6. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Qiskit's `Estimator` is used to directly compute expectation values of "
"Hamiltonians in VQE.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Multiple-choice questions: {#multiple-choice-questions}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "What is the purpose of the Hamiltonian in VQE?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A) To generate random quantum states\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "B) To determine the energy of quantum states\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "C) To optimize quantum circuits\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "D) To create entanglement\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "What is the primary objective of the VQE algorithm?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A) To find the ground state energy of a Hamiltonian\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "B) To create entanglement between qubits\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "C) To perform Grover's search\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "D) To break the RSA encryption\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '3. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"How many quantum states are generated in this notebook to compare the "
"ansatz?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A) 100\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "B) 1000\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "C) 5000\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "D) 10,000\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '4. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Why is a classical optimizer required in VQE?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A) To perform quantum measurements\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "B) Update ansatz parameters to minimize energy\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "C) To entangle qubits\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "D) To generate quantum randomness\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '5. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Why is the ansatz designed to be parameterized?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A) To allow quantum state preparation\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "B) To allow a wide space of quantum states to be searched\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "C) To reduce circuit complexity\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "D) To measure eigenvalues directly\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '6. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Which of the following is the most correct statement about choosing a good "
"ansatz?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A) An ansatz must produce states evenly distributed over the Bloch sphere, "
"or it will fail.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"B) An ansatz should be tailored to your system to make sure it can generate "
"states close to the ground state.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"C) An ansatz should produce random states using its variational "
"parameters.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "D) A better ansatz always has more variational parameters.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"(Optional) Appendix: Optimizer overhead by ansatz complexity {#optional-"
"appendix-optimizer-overhead-by-ansatz-complexity}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"VQE faces several well-known challenges[ref 6], and the following are "
"related to what we have learned above.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansatz selection challenges\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"There is an inherent challenge in selecting the right variational ansatz. "
"Chemistry-inspired ansätze (like UCCSD) provide physical accuracy but "
"require deep circuits, while hardware-efficient ansätze have shallower "
"circuits but may lack physical interpretability. Also, many ansätze "
"introduce excessive variational parameters that contribute little to "
"improving accuracy but significantly increase optimization difficulty.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Optimization difficulties\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The optimization landscape of VQE can have regions where gradients vanish "
"exponentially (barren plateaus), making it difficult for classical "
"optimizers to update the variational parameters efficiently. For this, "
"researchers have tried to use different types of optimizers - gradient-based"
" and gradient-free, but both face challenges. Gradient-based optimizers "
"suffer from barren plateaus, while gradient-free methods require a large "
"number of function evaluations.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '3. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Optimizer overhead\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"One more well-known challenge is optimizer overhead, which is related to the"
" scale of the problem. The quantum circuits required for VQE grow in depth "
"and complexity as the problem size increases; this typically also increases "
"the number of parameters to optimize. The optimization process becomes "
"intractable as the number of parameters increases, leading to slow "
"convergence and difficulties in finding the optimal solution.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Here we will take a look at these challenges by using VQE for a $H_2$ "
"molecule, with two different types of ansätze.\n"
msgstr ""

